10313|# THE flush (codex-telemetry.ps1 -Flush; the detached sender): under the sender lock
10314|# (Enter-TelemetryFlushLock - a concurrent sender is refused, a stale lock taken over), every spool
10315|# file oldest first: lines older than 7 days and lines that are not spool lines are dropped; events go
10316|# in batches of at most 100 ({"events": [...]}) to <base>/v2/events, complaints one by one to
10317|# <base>/v2/complaints; the delivered and dropped lines are removed, the rest stays.
10318|# (wave 28b, D2) One flush ends after 60 s in all (TEST HOOK, test mode only:
10319|# CODEX_CONSULT_TEST_TELEMETRY_FLUSH_MS), one request after 8 s (CODEX_CONSULT_TEST_TELEMETRY_REQUEST_MS)
10320|# - what is not sent stays in the spool. (D8) Against the intake as it is built: a batch refused with
10321|# 400 "events[i]: <reason>" - event i is DROPPED (one line in .last `rejected`) and the rest resent, at
10322|# most three times per flush; 413 - the batch is halved (an event refused alone is dropped); 403 - the
10323|# flush stops, the spool is kept, the reason is said; any other 4xx, a 5xx, a timeout - the flush
10324|# stops there (nothing is hammered; the next run's sender tries again). The events not spooled since
10325|# the last flush (D6) are counted from zero again.
10326|# (wave 28c, D5 / F42-8) The 60 s cover the WHOLE flush - the lock, the spool's enumeration, every
10327|# file's read, every request and every rewrite: the clock is checked before each step, and a step that
10328|# no longer fits stops the flush cleanly (what is not sent stays). A request is started only while
10329|# 1.5 s are left (1 s kept back for the rewrite that follows it); the rewrite of lines already
10330|# delivered is always attempted, with a wait of at most what is left (at least 0.1 s) - skipping it
10331|# would send them again. (D4) The sender checks its token in the lock before each send and each
10332|# rewrite: a sender that lost its lock stops without rewriting.
10333|# Writes <spool>/.last {time, result, delivered, kept, dropped, rejected, http}. { Exit (0 done or
10334|# nothing to send, 1 something not delivered or the URL refused, 2 another sender holds the lock);
10335|# Result; Delivered; Kept; Dropped; Rejected (string[]); Http; TookOver }.
